Third-party code vendored into @parle/index-codec
=================================================

Origin
------
@expo/binary-fuse-filter, version 1.0.0
Copyright (c) 2026 650 Industries, Inc. (Expo)
MIT — full text in ./LICENSE

That package is itself a port of the C reference implementation of binary fuse
filters by Thomas Mueller Graf and Daniel Lemire:
https://github.com/FastFilter/xor_singleheader
Apache-2.0 — full text in ./LICENSE-xor_singleheader

Both notices are retained here as those licences require. Both are compatible
with Parle's AGPL-3.0-only licensing.

Why it is vendored rather than depended on
------------------------------------------
The published package's only entry points take key STRINGS and hash them
internally with a non-standard FNV-1a-64 over UTF-16 code units. There is no way
to supply precomputed 64-bit keys. Depending on it would make Parle's artifact
format specifiable only as "whatever that npm build does to a string", which
ADR 0010 — anyone may rebuild these artifacts — does not permit, and which no
Rust or Python reimplementation could satisfy. The hash also has a real
collision family (the single code unit U+0100 collides with the pair
U+0000 U+0001), which is harmless for URLs but proves the function is a
specific build's behaviour rather than a named algorithm.

What was changed
----------------
1. Added `createBinaryFuseFilterFromKeys` and `binaryFuseFilterHasKey` in
   filter.ts, taking keys already reduced to 64 bits. Parle derives those bits
   itself from SHA-256 (see ../Key.ts) and uses only these.
2. Restored the TypeScript annotations the published build had erased, and
   converted `build/*.js` back to `.ts`.
3. Wrote every typed-array read as `a[i] ?? 0`, because this repo compiles with
   `noUncheckedIndexedAccess`. Every such read is in bounds by construction, so
   the fallback branch is dead.
4. In serialize.ts, replaced thrown `Error`s with a returned `Malformed` value.
   A corrupt artifact must degrade rather than propagate an exception into
   whichever fiber happened to be reading the index.
5. In serialize.ts, tightened two header checks on the READ path only: the body
   must be exactly the length its header describes rather than at least it, and
   the segment count must be at least one. A longer body is two blobs glued
   together and reading its prefix would adopt half an artifact silently; a zero
   segment count passes the geometry check but drives one of the three probe
   positions off the end of the fingerprint table. Neither can be produced by
   this file's own writer, so the byte format is unchanged.

Nothing else in the algorithm, the hashing, the seed sequence or the byte format
differs from upstream. The round-trip and no-false-negative properties are
checked in ./vendor.test.ts.
